        <h2 id="selection">How to Choose a Supplier in 4 Steps</h2>
        <p>
          Brand reputation alone will not pick your supplier. Work through these four steps in order, and most of the confusion disappears.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Lock the application spec.</strong> Write down the fluid, its chemical compatibility needs, temperature, viscosity and solids content, required pressure, the flow band, and the duty cycle. The tube chemistry and head class you need fall out of this list — see our tube guide for the material mapping.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Size the technology, not the brand.</strong> Decide whether you need a standard head, a precision/industrial head, fixed or variable speed, and whether the drive must accept external control signals. This defines the product category before any supplier comparison.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Screen suppliers beyond price.</strong> Ask whether you are dealing with a manufacturer or a trading company, whether OEM/ODM is available, what quality certifications apply, and what lead times and spare-parts commitments look like. A supplier who cannot answer these questions clearly is a risk at any price.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Validate with samples and total cost.</strong> Run a trial with your actual fluid, measure tube life under your duty, and compare total cost of ownership — purchase price, consumables, spares and downtime — over a realistic horizon. The cheapest pump is rarely the cheapest to run.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
        <h3>Are Chinese peristaltic pump manufacturers reliable?</h3>
        <p>
          Many Chinese manufacturers — especially around the Baoding cluster — have years of export and OEM experience and can be dependable partners. Quality varies from factory to factory, so the reliable approach is the same as with any supplier: check certifications, request samples, run trials and, where volumes justify it, audit the factory or use a third-party inspection.
        </p>
        <h3>Which brand should a laboratory choose?</h3>
        <p>
          It depends on budget and support needs. Established Western brands such as Watson-Marlow and Masterflex offer convenience, documentation and local support at a higher price. Value-oriented options such as Lead Fluid, Longer and Kamoer cover most lab duties at a fraction of the cost. For research-grade work with strict validation requirements, the premium route is often worth it.
        </p>
        <h3>Do I need OEM customization?</h3>
        <p>
          OEM/ODM matters when you sell pumps under your own brand, need a specific head-and-drive combination, or integrate the pump into equipment that must look and behave like part of your product. Chinese suppliers such as Chonry and the Baoding OEM factories are built for this; catalog-only suppliers are usually less flexible.
        </p>
        <h3>What should I check before importing pumps from China?</h3>
        <p>
          Confirm the tube chemistry for your fluid before ordering, request samples rather than buying on spec, agree on lead times and spare parts in writing, and verify the factory is the actual manufacturer rather than a reseller. A short, well-run trial protects you from the most common surprises.
        </p>

        <h2 id="final-word">Final Word</h2>
        <p>
          No single manufacturer suits every buyer, and the profiles above are starting points, not verdicts. If your application is regulated and high-value, a premium Western brand earns its price. If you are building a product, a catalog or a distribution line, the Chinese OEM cluster offers capabilities that are hard to match on cost. And if you want a supplier that behaves like a manufacturer — transparent about tubing, heads and lead times, and comfortable with OEM/ODM — that is exactly the conversation we are set up for.
        </p>
